# Picks the Law of the Day at 00:00 UTC, so it is chosen on a schedule and not by the first visitor of the day.
# The deploy installs this file as /etc/cron.d/murphys-laws-law-of-day (backend/scripts/install-law-of-day-cron.sh),
# so change it in the repository: an edit made on the server is overwritten by the next deploy. A name in
# /etc/cron.d must not contain a dot, or cron ignores the file.
# backend/scripts/run-cron-job.sh runs the job from the backend directory, appends its output to backend/logs/law-of-day.log
# (backend/logs, which the deploy creates and keeps), records each run in backend/logs/cron-state/ (the daily report flags a
# job that never ran) and emails an alert when the job fails. The job starts the same tsx entry point as PM2 does
# for the API. The installer writes the absolute path of the node the deploy runs with (the nvm node, LAW_OF_DAY_NODE_BIN)
# in place of /usr/bin/node below. /usr/bin/node stays the default only for a caller that sets no node; on the production
# server it is a symlink to nvm v22.20.0 (the node PM2 runs), while the deploy's node is v22.16.0, the version of .nvmrc.
# The installer refuses to install this file when the node it would run is not the major Node version of .nvmrc or cannot
# load better-sqlite3.
# The Debian and Ubuntu cron package (the production server's cron) ignores CRON_TZ: its crontab(5) documents only TZ,
# which sets the job's environment and not its schedule. So the job runs in the server's own time zone, which is UTC
# (Etc/UTC), and the installer prints a warning at deploy time when it is not. The CRON_TZ=UTC line is kept because it is
# harmless there and is honored by cronie on a host that uses it. The pick is always for the current UTC day, so a run at another hour
# still picks the right law.
SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
CRON_TZ=UTC
0 0 * * * root /root/murphys-laws/backend/scripts/run-cron-job.sh law-of-day /usr/bin/node node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs scripts/select-law-of-day.ts
